facts.fact:
4th place ('Too much ...', AleXXL1986) used no ML model. Their notebook says, in Russian:
1. drop user-show views with watch share below 0.5;
2. project the user's viewing week (channel x weekday x time sessions) onto the future schedule;
3. take the programmes the user would watch most often if they kept the same routine;
4. pad with globally popular shows;
5. restricting to shows the user had already watched raised the score.
Stated scores: 'Private 0.42544 / 4 место; Public 0.43511 / 4 место' (the team's best public was 0.43566). Local validation (train to about 2020-05-17, validate the rest, users in both): global top-5 0.2308, per-user >=0.8 frequency 0.2545, schedule projection plus fallbacks 0.2720, blend 0.2654.

10th place (Gornyaki: Oleksii Tsepa, Myhaylo Turkin; the README also lists Andriy Samoshyn) scored private 0.40861, public 0.42183. Method:
- keep airings with view_pct >= 0.8 and schedule duration < 10000 s; drop the 'Фильмы' (films) category;
- weight each view by 5 if fully watched, else 2+2x^2, times 0.8^(days/60);
- score = count x mean weight x log10(number of past airings);
- drop shows absent from the test schedule;
- fill gaps from the 10 most similar users (cosine), then global top.
Validation: March-June features vs July target, keeping only users with exactly 5 target shows. Result: ml_metrics MAPK 0.3269.
